line-depth
==========

Print the *commit depth* of lines of a file: how many distinct commits in a
revision range touched a line, taking the largest value over the lines
selected.

    line-depth [options] <rev-range> <start>[,<end>]:<file>
    line-depth [options] <rev-range> max:<file>

For example, `line-depth origin/main..HEAD 10,40:foo.c` prints the highest
depth among lines 10 to 40 of `foo.c` as of `HEAD`, and `max:foo.c` covers
every line of the file.  The only output on success is one integer.

Options: `--fast` (below), `--first-parent` (count only the first-parent
chain, as for `git log --first-parent`: a merge that differs from its first
parent then counts, and the side branch does not), and
`--ignore-revs-file=<file>` (leave the listed commits out of every count; one
full commit name per line, `#` comments and blank lines allowed, like blame's
ignore-revs files, for mass reformatting commits; annotated tag names are not
peeled, as blame does).

How it works
------------

Nothing here tracks history itself.  The depth of a line is the number of
distinct commits that `git log -L<n>,<n>:<file> <rev-range>` reports, and the
result for a range or file is the maximum of those per-line depths -- not the
number of commits touching the range as a whole.  Two lines edited by two
different commits therefore give 1, not 2.  The commit that adds a line
counts as a touch.

To avoid one `git log -L` run per line, it runs `git log -L` over a whole range
of lines first.  The commits reported for the range are an upper bound on the
depth of every line in it, so a range that cannot beat the best depth seen so
far is skipped, and any other range is split in halves until single lines
remain.  A file in which every line was touched once needs only about twice
log2(lines) runs, not one per line.

What it does not cover
----------------------

- Line numbers refer to the file at the tip of `<rev-range>`.  Lines deleted
  before the tip, and lines added and removed inside the range, are not
  measured.  A depth of 0 for an emptied file does not say anything about the
  commits that deleted its lines.
- It follows Git's `-L` history, including its merge handling: when a merge
  matches one parent for the line, only that parent is followed, so side
  commits can go uncounted.  Counts can also be too high: when a hunk is
  replaced, split or joined, every resulting line inherits the history of the
  whole old hunk.
- Renames are followed according to `diff.renames`, so the same history can
  give different numbers under different configuration.
- Binary files are rejected.  Symbolic links and submodules, and shallow
  repositories (history cut off at the shallow boundary undercounts), are not
  handled specially yet.
- Only plain numbers are accepted for `<start>` and `<end>`.  As with
  `git log -L`, reversed endpoints are swapped and an end past the end of the
  file is clamped; a start past the end of the file is an error.

An empty revision set (for example `X..X`) prints 0 once the revisions, path
and lines have been validated.  Any failure from Git, such as an unknown
revision or a missing path, makes the command fail with a message and no
output on stdout.

For reviewing a branch, use the tip of the base branch as the left side
(`origin/main..HEAD`).  Using the merge base instead also counts commits from
the base that were merged into the branch.

Speed: `--fast`
---------------

With `--fast` only the lines that `git diff -U0 X Y` reports as added or
modified are searched, one range per changed hunk, since any other line has
depth 0.  Every such line has depth at least 1, so a hunk whose commits number
exactly 1 is settled by a single run.  This needs a plain `X..Y` range (not
`X...Y` or a single revision).

On a 1210-line file over 3000 commits (answer 2) the default takes about 9
seconds and `--fast` about 4, against roughly 90 seconds for one `git log -L`
run per line.

The result differs from the default in one case: a line changed and then
restored to its original text within the range is not in that diff, so
`--fast` leaves it out while the default counts it.  Use the default when
such lines matter.  Renames are not followed when listing candidate lines,
so a renamed file simply yields all its lines as candidates.

touch-footprint
---------------

`line-depth` only sees lines that survive in the tip.  Its companion
`touch-footprint <X..Y> [<path>...]` looks at the other side: the lines of `X`
that the range modifies or deletes, taken from `git diff -U0 X Y` and blamed
at `X`.  Blame costs about 0.15 seconds per file, so `--jobs=<n>` runs that
many blames at once through `xargs -P` (22 seconds became 4.4 with 14 jobs on
244 files, with identical output); the default is 1.  It prints one `key value`
pair per line:

    lines        how many such lines there are
    commits      distinct commits that last touched them
    authors      distinct authors (by email)
    oldest_days  age of the oldest line at X
    median_days  age of the median line at X (the lower one if the count is even)

For example, over 60 commits of this repository it reports 2068 changed or
removed lines last touched by 485 commits and 103 authors, the oldest 7752 days
old, in about 20 seconds.

Blame reports only the *last* commit to touch each line, so this says who owns
the code being disturbed, not how often it changed; use `line-depth` over an
earlier range (`X~N..X`) for that.  Lines added by the range have no history
and a line changed and then restored inside the range is not in the net diff.
Renames are followed (a pure rename has no footprint), `blame.ignoreRevsFile`
and similar user configuration apply, and paths that need quoting are not
supported.

Other measurements
------------------

Each of these is a separate script with the same conventions: it takes an
`X..Y` range (the tip of the base branch on the left), optional paths, and
prints `key value` lines, with no policy attached.  They are built from
existing Git commands.

Where a script compares the two ends (`churn-ratio`, `change-spread`,
`moved-lines`, `touch-footprint`, `forward-survival`, `pre-range-depth` and
`line-depth --fast`) it diffs the *trees* of `X` and `Y`, while the commit
counts use the commits of `X..Y`.  For a branch that has not been rebased onto
`X` the tree diff also contains what the mainline did since the fork, so pass
the merge base as `X` for those: `$(git merge-base base head)..head`.  If the
branch merged another topic that the base also merged there can be several
merge bases (`git merge-base --all`), and no single one is right; in a study of
60 topics here that happened for the first one examined, where the tree diff
said 4770 lines changed and the merge-base diff 1465, for 6 commits that changed
257.  The `X...Y` spelling is rejected for this reason rather than guessed.

`churn-ratio <X..Y> [<path>...]`::
	`gross` lines changed by the commits of the range (`git log --numstat`),
	`net` lines changed by one diff of the two ends, and `rework`, the
	surplus.

`change-spread <X..Y> [<path>...]`::
	`commits`, `merges`, `authors`, `files`, `dirs`, `subsystems`,
	`functions` (from hunk headers) and the `entropy` in bits of the lines
	changed per file.

`merge-effort <rev1> <rev2>`::
	What merging two commits would take: `merge_bases`, `left_only`,
	`right_only`, `files_left`, `files_right`, `files_both` and the
	`conflicts` found by `git merge-tree --write-tree` (which writes only
	objects).

`merge-resolution <merge-commit>`::
	The hand edits in an existing merge: `parents`, and the `files`,
	`added` and `deleted` lines that differ from an automatic re-merge
	(`git diff-tree --remerge-diff`).  Conflict markers count as deleted
	lines.  Git defines remerge only for two-parent merges and says its
	output may change, so an octopus merge is refused.  Needs Git 2.46.1 or
	later: before that `git diff-tree --remerge-diff` crashed.

`pre-range-depth <W> <X..Y> [<path>...]`::
	How contested the lines the range changes already were: `line-depth`
	over `W..X`, the window before the range, for the old-side lines of
	each hunk.  Reports `ranges` and the largest `depth`.

`forward-survival [--jobs=<n>] <X..Y> [<path>...]`::
	`git blame --reverse` from `X`: `files`, `lines`, the number
	`removed` by `Y`, and the distinct revisions (`removal_points`) at which
	the last of them were still present.  Reverse blame names the last
	revision that still held a line, not the commit that removed it, and
	after merges it can point at the commit where branches diverged, so
	`removal_points` counts places in history, not removal events.

`moved-lines <X..Y> [<path>...]`::
	`added` and `removed` lines, and how many are `moved_added` and
	`moved_removed` according to `git diff --color-moved=zebra` (blocks of at
	least 20 alphanumeric characters).  Needs Git 2.46.0 or later: before
	that `--color-moved` was silently ignored whenever `diff.external` was
	set, even with `--no-ext-diff`.

Function-level depth needs no script: `git log -L :<funcname>:<file> --format=%H
--no-patch X..Y | wc -l` counts the commits that touched a function (it fails
for `X..X`, and counts commits that touch any line of the function, not the
deepest line).

Tests
-----

    make -C t

The tests in `t/t9420-line-depth-characterize.sh` run `git log -L` alone and
record the behavior `line-depth` relies on.  Run them against the Git you use:
`-L`'s idea of a touch has changed over time and results depend on the
version.  They were last run with Git built from upstream master `37b3475fb5`.
